+**Photo swap.** Page-wide ELA rarely notices a replaced photo: the photo is a few percent
+of the page. So the photo frame (the field detector's `photo` box, or the face box grown to
+a photo frame) is compared with the rest of the card: re-compression error per unit of edge
+strength, inside over outside. A pasted photo carries its own compression history and scores
+well above a printed one; a genuine card forwarded over WhatsApp scores lower, not higher.
+At or above `PORTRAIT_ELA_RATIO_LIMIT` the forensics score is at least 60 (tampered).
+
+**Same face, another identity.** Every document portrait is kept as a 128-number SFace
+embedding (no image) in a local index with the holder details read off that card, and expires
+with the scan. A new portrait close to a stored one (cosine ≥ 0.60; this is a 1:N search, so
+the bar is well above the 1:1 band of 0.45) under a different name or date of birth raises
+`FACE_UNDER_OTHER_IDENTITY`: critical at ≥ 0.70, a warning below. Same face with the same name
+and date of birth is the same person on another document and raises nothing.
+
 **Worked example** (the tampered passport in the first screenshot, no live photo):
 
